Q AND A FROM SESSION:

Q: What is a Namespace?
A: An isolated environment used to separate workloads like dev, pre-prod, prod, experimental, etc.

Q: What is written in secrets.yml?
A: A secret named "DB Secret" containing two database credentials.

Q: What does "Opaque" type mean in secrets?
A: Opaque means not visible or see-through, indicating sensitive data that is hidden/blocked.

Q: What is a Config Map?
A: A file storing non-sensitive configuration information, such as endpoints or URLs, used to connect applications within Kubernetes.

Q: How do applications connect in Kubernetes?
A: Either through labels and selectors, or through config maps that provide endpoint/URL information.


Deploy Application (NGINX)
Deploy NGINX with 2 replicas. The deployment YAML references both the ConfigMap and Secrets.
Commands: kubectl apply, kubectl get pods, kubectl get deployments.

Service
A service acts as a proxy/connector to expose the deployment internally within the cluster (ClusterIP, not LoadBalancer this time).

Ingress
Ingress manages routing rules for incoming traffic to reach the application. It avoids the need for multiple external IPs (which are chargeable). GCP has a built-in Ingress controller. Other controllers like NGINX Ingress are also available.

Port Forwarding
Since no external IP is assigned, use port forwarding to access the app via the browser using Google Cloud Shell Web Preview.

Scaling
To scale replicas without UI access, use:
kubectl scale deployment demo-service --replicas=5
Important note: Scaling may cause version mismatch between old and new pods, so a rollout of the new version is needed when application changes occur.


What is a Namespace? - It is an isolated environment used to separate different environments like dev, pre-prod, and prod.

What is a Secret? - It stores sensitive information like database credentials. The type "Opaque" means the data is not visible or see-through, indicating sensitive content encoded in base64.

What is a ConfigMap? - It stores non-sensitive configuration information, such as database URLs and application endpoints, used to connect applications within the cluster.

What is Ingress? - It defines routing rules for how external or internal traffic flows into the application. It avoids the need for multiple external IPs (which are chargeable) by routing traffic through a single controller.

Why create a Service? - A Service acts as a proxy/connector to expose pods and make them accessible within or outside the cluster.

How to scale an application without UI access? - Use the kubectl scale command and specify the desired number of replicas.

What is the problem when scaling? - When new pods are created after scaling, some pods may run on the old version while others run on the new version, causing inconsistency.

How to fix version inconsistency after scaling? - Use the kubectl rollout command to push the new version to all pods uniformly.

What is the difference between create and apply commands? - Best practice is to use create for the first deployment and apply for subsequent updates.

How to view a running resource as YAML? - Add the flag -o yaml to your kubectl command to see the YAML definition of any running resource.

